╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ rank_quantile_reweighting                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The generated item weights follow an exponential distribution. The existing pivot-gap     │
│                          │ reconstruction attempts to infer numerical differences from `1 vs 2` comparisons, but     │
│                          │ these estimates are noisy and can become distorted by sparse/random pivot spacing.        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ After all item-to-pivot binary searches, the program knows a much more reliable signal:   │
│                          │ the ordinal interval of every item among the sorted pivots. This edit records those       │
│                          │ intervals and recalibrates estimated weights from their implied ranks using the           │
│                          │ exponential quantile function `-log(1-p)`. It retains the existing estimator for very     │
│                          │ small pivot counts, while for at least four pivots it uses the distribution information   │
│                          │ explicitly supplied by the input generator. Items in the same unresolved interval receive │
│                          │ the interval's expected rank, which is preferable to arbitrary geometric interpolation    │
│                          │ for exponential data.                                                                     │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0496                                                                                   │
